(1814536, 244857, 40371, '<td valign="top">It truely sickens me that you were placed this far back.  This is a spectacular picture.  No apologies needed for the title, I thought it was well executed.</td>', 'It truely sickens me that you were placed this far back.  This is a spectacular picture.  No apologies needed for the title, I thought it was well executed.', '2005-10-26 12:34:31.000000', NULL, 0),
(1814549, 246157, 40371, '<td valign="top">How is it that you placed so far back.  Your reflection is nearly flawless and the leaves break it up and give a real abstract feel.  Maybe cropping a little more off the top would have helped, who knows?  I gave you a 9.  Excellent creativity.</td>', 'How is it that you placed so far back.  Your reflection is nearly flawless and the leaves break it up and give a real abstract feel.  Maybe cropping a little more off the top would have helped, who knows?  I gave you a 9.  Excellent creativity.', '2005-10-26 12:37:02.000000', NULL, 0),
(1814553, 245319, 6999, '<td valign="top">This has major potential, I think. There are photographs by Andre Kertesz from the time he spent in New York (an unhappy decade for him, really) that use just such a technique of refelctions in puddles to break the sense of the ordinary in his images. I hate the comments I recieve suggesting different framing of my photos, and usually hate to make such comments, but in this case I think my feeling is so strong I must do so: you don''t need to see the ''real bridge'' in this shot. Did you try a horozontal framing, from maybe a step to the right, to play on the contrast beteen the tarmac and the cobbles and the water (especially the cobbles disappearing into the water), and to frame the refelcted support of the bridge more fully in the water? Whilst I think I understand the more purely documentary approach you''ve taken, I do think the reflection is so understated here that it was never going to succeed score-wise. <br/> <br/>However; just as others have said, what''s important is what you are trying to communicate.</td>', 'This has major potential, I think. There are photographs by Andre Kertesz from the time he spent in New York (an unhappy decade for him, really) that use just such a technique of refelctions in puddles to break the sense of the ordinary in his images. I hate the comments I recieve suggesting different framing of my photos, and usually hate to make such comments, but in this case I think my feeling is so strong I must do so: you don''t need to see the ''real bridge'' in this shot. Did you try a horozontal framing, from maybe a step to the right, to play on the contrast beteen the tarmac and the cobbles and the water (especially the cobbles disappearing into the water), and to frame the refelcted support of the bridge more fully in the water? Whilst I think I understand the more purely documentary approach you''ve taken, I do think the reflection is so understated here that it was never going to succeed score-wise.  However; just as others have said, what''s important is what you are trying to communicate.', '2005-10-26 12:38:36.000000', NULL, 0),
(1814556, 243883, 40371, '<td valign="top">What on Earth is wrong with the voters!?!  This was an absolute 10 in my book.  So much creativity and vision in something most people would just step over.  It appears it got stepped over in the voting as well.</td>', 'What on Earth is wrong with the voters!?!  This was an absolute 10 in my book.  So much creativity and vision in something most people would just step over.  It appears it got stepped over in the voting as well.', '2005-10-26 12:39:19.000000', NULL, 0),
(1814560, 243894, 10634, '<td valign="top">I think this is a very nice entry. I think it should have done a little  better then it did... maybe a 5.6-5.8. I did not vote in this challenge, but probably would have give you  a 7. You''re right that abstracts are a hard sell here, but I have found they are hard to sell at most of the "challenge" sites. Balancing the light is also a good idea. The first thing my eye is drawn to is the bright spot in the top left. Otherwise, I like it. :-)</td>', 'I think this is a very nice entry. I think it should have done a little  better then it did... maybe a 5.6-5.8. I did not vote in this challenge, but probably would have give you  a 7. You''re right that abstracts are a hard sell here, but I have found they are hard to sell at most of the "challenge" sites. Balancing the light is also a good idea. The first thing my eye is drawn to is the bright spot in the top left. Otherwise, I like it. :-)', '2005-10-26 12:40:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(1814563, 246303, 40371, '<td valign="top">So you have all these great comments but yet mostly average votes.  This should have been higher on creativity alone plus the person in the upper right gives the shot a certain irony.</td>', 'So you have all these great comments but yet mostly average votes.  This should have been higher on creativity alone plus the person in the upper right gives the shot a certain irony.', '2005-10-26 12:42:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(1814570, 244224, 40371, '<td valign="top">People are way too anal sometimes.  So what if it wasn''t an "exact" reflection.  I gave you a 9.</td>', 'People are way too anal sometimes.  So what if it wasn''t an "exact" reflection.  I gave you a 9.', '2005-10-26 12:44:48.000000', NULL, 0),
(1814577, 245414, 35602, '<td valign="top">I did not vote or enter this challenge (Computer was down) but I think the other comments do tell the story to what is wrong with the image.  It does not really draw the attention of viewers, because most won''t look at an image for more than 2 seconds, hit a number then move on to the next one.  It is light and dark, and a water reflection, but you also need to see what you were up against, which really guides many of the votes as well.  If you voted on this one, see which ones you gave higher marks to, ask yourself why, then look at yours as just another image amongst the many.  That is what I have been trying to do, but it can be frustrating to say the least.</td>', 'I did not vote or enter this challenge (Computer was down) but I think the other comments do tell the story to what is wrong with the image.  It does not really draw the attention of viewers, because most won''t look at an image for more than 2 seconds, hit a number then move on to the next one.  It is light and dark, and a water reflection, but you also need to see what you were up against, which really guides many of the votes as well.  If you voted on this one, see which ones you gave higher marks to, ask yourself why, then look at yours as just another image amongst the many.  That is what I have been trying to do, but it can be frustrating to say the least.', '2005-10-26 12:46:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(1814579, 243894, 40371, '<td valign="top">I gave you an 8, if I had time to re-evaluate I would have upped you to a 9.  I have noticed that people on this site have a decidedly dry sense of humor; it''s refreshing that yours isn''t.</td>', 'I gave you an 8, if I had time to re-evaluate I would have upped you to a 9.  I have noticed that people on this site have a decidedly dry sense of humor; it''s refreshing that yours isn''t.', '2005-10-26 12:47:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(1814597, 244353, 28710, '<td valign="top">Wow. Looking at this picture made me try to think of the thought process to come up with this idea. Well Done! On your second entry too!<br/> <br/>Stunning matchup of reflection tomato to background tomato.<br/> <br/>It is nice to see a ribbon for an Icelander that people won''t be able to attribute to the local landscape. Maybe it''s something in the water?<br/> <br/>PS. Black Market Icelandic water available for $500 for a 950mL bottle. Works wonders on tomato plants too! Irrigation project speculators welcome!<br/> <br/></td>', 'Wow. Looking at this picture made me try to think of the thought process to come up with this idea. Well Done! On your second entry too! Stunning matchup of reflection tomato to background tomato. It is nice to see a ribbon for an Icelander that people won''t be able to attribute to the local landscape. Maybe it''s something in the water? PS. Black Market Icelandic water available for $500 for a 950mL bottle. Works wonders on tomato plants too! Irrigation project speculators welcome! ', '2005-10-26 12:55:22.000000', NULL, 0),
